<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zuo Zhuan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zuozhuan, Commentary of Zuo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zuo Zhuan is an ancient Chinese narrative history covering the period from 722 to 468 BCE, arranged as a commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals of the State of Lu. Traditionally attributed to Zuo Qiuming, it was probably compiled in the fourth century BCE. It is the main source for the politics, wars and diplomacy of the Spring and Autumn period and a model of classical Chinese prose.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan.jpg" alt="Zuo Zhuan" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chang Wei-tung · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Yuanyang_Zuo_zhuan_first_page.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Little is known of how the work came into being. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Grand Historian, of the 1st century BCE, calls it Master Zuo's Spring and Autumn Annals and says that Zuo compiled it from the scribal records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> to preserve the true teachings. The identification of this Zuo with the Zuo Qiuming praised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> rests on no specific evidence and was challenged as early as the 8th century. Excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and analysis of its language suggest that its composition was largely complete by 300 BCE.</p><p>In the early 19th century Liu Fenglu argued that the text was not originally a commentary on the Annals, a view now generally accepted. Kang Youwei went further and held that the Han scholar Liu Xin had forged it; that theory is now considered discredited. The present arrangement, in which each entry of the Annals is followed by the matching narrative, was made by Du Yu in the 3rd century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work has 30 chapters and tells its events from the perspective of the State of Lu. Each year opens with the terse entry of the Annals, followed by narratives that are often long and detailed: battles, assassinations, intrigue, oppression and uprisings, ghosts and portents. Because the order is strictly chronological, connected episodes may be separated by the events of intervening years. Its account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chengpu">Battle of Chengpu</a> in 632 BCE, in which the State of Jin under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin">Chong'er</a> defeated the State of Chu, is typical in giving more space to preparations, omens and the division of spoils than to the fighting.</p><p>The narration stays in the third person and conveys its lessons through speech and action: the haughty and wicked generally bring disaster on themselves, and ritual propriety governs all conduct, including war, though the good are also shown suffering senseless violence. Many anecdotes end with a brief verdict attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> or to an unnamed gentleman; these were added later by Confucian scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The work was canonised as one of the Chinese classics in the 1st century CE and remained a cornerstone of education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> until modern times. It is the source of more chengyu, or set idioms, than any other work, and the Qing anthology Guwen Guanzhi took 34 passages from it, more than from any other source. The oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are six fragments found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> manuscripts by Paul Pelliot, dating from the 3rd to the 7th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zuo Zhuan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Deng is known mainly through the <b>Zuo zhuan</b>, which notes marriages between its ruling house and the kings of Chu: Deng Man was the wife of King Wu of Chu and mother of King Wen.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deng">State of Deng</a></li><li><blockquote>Jiang appears in the <b>Zuo zhuan</b> when, with the State of Huang, it joined the covenant led by Duke Huan of Qi in the 650s BCE, a gesture of defiance towards the State of Chu, its powerful southern neighbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-jiang">State of Jiang</a></li><li><blockquote>Ju appears often in the <b>Zuo zhuan</b> as a middling power that fought and allied with its neighbours the State of Lu and the State of Qi.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-ju">State of Ju</a></li><li><blockquote>Xi appears in the <b>Zuo zhuan</b> chiefly in a story of feuding: the Marquis of Xi, angered that the Marquis of Cai had insulted his wife, Xi Gui, invited Chu to attack Cai, setting off a chain of events that drew Chu into the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-xi">State of Xi</a></li><li><blockquote>Little is known of its internal life beyond the bronzes and brief entries in the <b>Zuo zhuan</b>, which show a typical Zhou aristocratic state organised around its ruling lineage and ancestral temple.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-xing">State of Xing</a></li><li><blockquote>Little else is known of its institutions beyond the episode that ended it, recorded in the <b>Zuo zhuan</b> and Gongyang commentary.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-yu">State of Yu</a></li></ul></section>
